ENCAPSULATION in a single class: the class bundles data (fields) with methods that operate on that data, and restricts access using access modifiers. Private fields cannot be read or written from outside the class without going through public methods that validate and control the access.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    // ENCAPSULATION: private fields — hidden from outside
    private final String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private final List<String> transactionLog = new ArrayList<>();

    // Balance can ONLY be modified through these controlled methods:
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        validatePositive(amount);
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        transactionLog.add("CREDIT: Rs." + amount);
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    // NO setBalance() — balance is FULLY encapsulated!
}
```

ABSTRACTION in a single class: the class has public methods that describe WHAT the class does, and private methods that contain HOW it does it. The caller never sees the internal implementation. If the internal logic changes, the public interface stays the same.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    // ═══ ABSTRACTION: public methods = WHAT (interface) ═══
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        validatePositive(amount);        // step 1: validation
        updateBalance(amount);           // step 2: balance change
        logTransaction("CREDIT", amount);// step 3: audit
        notifyHolder("Deposit", amount); // step 4: notification
    }
    // Caller sees: deposit(amount). That's it. WHAT = "deposit money."

    // ═══ ABSTRACTION: private methods = HOW (hidden implementation) ═══
    private void validatePositive(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Must be positive");
    }
    private void updateBalance(BigDecimal amount) { balance = balance.add(amount); }
    private void logTransaction(String type, BigDecimal amount) {
        transactionLog.add(type + ": Rs." + amount + " at " + LocalDateTime.now());
    }
    private void notifyHolder(String action, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Send SMS, email, push notification — caller doesn't know or care
    }
    // These 4 methods are HIDDEN from the caller — abstraction!
    // Tomorrow: change logTransaction to write to Kafka → caller unchanged.
}
```

POLYMORPHISM (compile-time) in a single class via METHOD OVERLOADING: multiple methods with the same name but different parameters. The compiler decides which version to call based on the argument types at compile time.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    // ═══ POLYMORPHISM via OVERLOADING — same name, different signatures ═══

    // Version 1: deposit with just amount
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        validatePositive(amount);
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        logTransaction("CREDIT", amount, "No narration");
    }

    // Version 2: deposit with amount + narration
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount, String narration) {
        validatePositive(amount);
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        logTransaction("CREDIT", amount, narration);
    }

    // Version 3: deposit with amount + narration + reference number
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount, String narration, String referenceNo) {
        validatePositive(amount);
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        logTransaction("CREDIT", amount, narration + " [REF:" + referenceNo + "]");
    }

    // THREE methods named "deposit" — the compiler picks the right one
    // based on arguments at compile time. This IS polymorphism!
    // account.deposit(new BigDecimal("5000"));
    // account.deposit(new BigDecimal("5000"), "Salary");
    // account.deposit(new BigDecimal("5000"), "Salary", "NEFT-UTR-123");
}
```

INHERITANCE in a single class via implicit `extends Object`: every Java class that doesn't explicitly extend another class implicitly extends `java.lang.Object`. Object provides 11 methods that every class inherits. OVERRIDING these methods proves RUNTIME polymorphism even in a single class.

```java
public class BankAccount /* implicitly extends Object */ {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // ═══ INHERITANCE from Object — and OVERRIDING (runtime polymorphism) ═══

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        // Overrides Object.toString() which returns "com.bank.BankAccount@1a2b3c"
        return accountId + ": Rs." + balance;
        // Now: "SAV2001: Rs.125000"
        // This is RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM: Object ref calling toString() dispatches to our version
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object other) {
        // Overrides Object.equals() which compares references (==)
        if (this == other) return true;
        if (!(other instanceof BankAccount that)) return false;
        return Objects.equals(accountId, that.accountId);
        // Now two accounts with same accountId are EQUAL (business logic)
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        // Must override when equals is overridden (contract!)
        return Objects.hash(accountId);
    }

    // PROOF of runtime polymorphism:
    // Object ref = new BankAccount("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"));
    // ref.toString();  // calls BankAccount.toString(), NOT Object.toString()!
    // The JVM dispatches to the ACTUAL type at runtime — this IS polymorphism.
}
```

